  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nScattered rain showers move into the area later this evening and \ninto tonight. Coverage begins over central South Dakota before \nexpanding to the rest of the forecast area during the overnight \nperiod. Generally expecting rain rates to remain fairly light \noverall, but under the strongest showers, around a tenth of an inch \nmay fall in total (roughly a 25-45% chance over parts of \nnortheastern South Dakota). \n\nWith an upper-level ridge overhead, above normal temperatures can be \nexpected for the next couple of days. Highs in the 50s to low 60s \nare about 5-10 degrees above normal for this time of year. Low \ntemperatures are also expected to stay well above freezing through \nthe weekend. The upper-level pattern will shift at the start of next \nweek, and an upper-level trough will move overhead. This will push \ntemperatures back down closer to normal (highs in the low 50s to \nupper 40s) for mid to late next week, as well as bring back chances \nfor freezing temperatures during the overnights. \n\nWith the coming pattern change, a Colorado Low is set to move into \nthe region early next week, bringing another round of rainfall to \nthe region. Rainfall totals over the last 24 hours of model runs \nhave continued to trend upwards. The footprint of the area of \nheaviest rainfall has also shifted, now located firmly over \nnortheastern South Dakota and western Minnesota. In that area, \nensemble median rainfall totals through Wednesday morning are up to \n2\". While the GEFS and NBM have their highest rainfall totals over \nparts of the Aberdeen forecast area, the Euro ensemble favors a more \neasterly track of the low, which would lead to a slight decrease in \nthe precipitation potential should that scenario pan out (medians \naround 1.5\"). In either scenario, rainfall totals decrease moving to \nthe west. As the low pressure center moves in and tracks north, \nwinds will turn northwesterly and increase thanks to the tightening \npressure gradient. The strongest gusts are expected along and west \nof the Missouri River Monday afternoon through Tuesday morning, \nwhere gusts have a 30-60% chance to hit Wind Advisory criteria of 45 \nmiles per hour or higher.",
